WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.160
Film Courage: What elements make up a great story?

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:05.440
Pat Verducci, Writing Coach, Teacher and Mentor:&nbsp;
Well I think when you're writing and you sort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.080
of have an idea and you go Wow, will this&nbsp;
actually make a great feature film or not?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:15.920
I usually think about Okay, are these three&nbsp;
elements part of the idea or could I find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:21.440
a way to sort of embed these three elements?&nbsp;
One is that it needs to have a main character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:28.960
who wants something really badly. The second&nbsp;
one is it has to promise lots of conflict.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.960
When we imagine the story and we imagine what the&nbsp;
character wants and what they're going to have to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:39.840
do to get it, we already start to kind of imagine&nbsp;
all the crazy obstacles that that person's going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:46.160
to have to face in order to get what they want and&nbsp;
then we get really interested right? Then third I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:52.960
think a way to tell if your idea will make a&nbsp;
great movie is does it have human emotions in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:56.720
it that we can relate to you know even if you're&nbsp;
writing about science fiction or you're writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.720 --> 00:01:04.160
a horror movie or something set way in the past&nbsp;
where you really have no immediate connection to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:10.400
the time or place or characters there have to&nbsp;
be those human feelings and desires and fears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:15.760
that we can relate to you know so I always&nbsp;
think those are the three criteria that make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.760 --> 00:01:21.360
a great movie or tv idea and obviously if&nbsp;
you're writing a tv show you know the idea has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:26.880
to promise lots of story you know when you're&nbsp;
writing a feature it's a contained three-act&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:33.840
structure right so it has it's a contained story&nbsp;
with an end so you know thinking about that idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:38.400
all those elements still have to be at play and&nbsp;
then in addition when you're writing a tv limited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:45.680
series or a tv show with multiple seasons you&nbsp;
know how are all those characters and all those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:53.760
conflicts going to spool out and promise lots of&nbsp;
conflict to come yeah great great three tips first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.760 --> 00:02:00.480
off how would we know that the characters want&nbsp;
is really strong enough that it warrants us being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.480 --> 00:02:08.000
invested in them right well as a story consultant&nbsp;
I mean I work with a lot of screenwriters I work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.400
with professional screenwriters who are working in&nbsp;
the industry and I also work with beginning people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:17.360
and people who are kind of in the middle of their&nbsp;
journey toward being a professional screenwriter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:24.080
but even with the professional screenwriters that&nbsp;
I work with it's always the same it's like every&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:32.320
time I read the script it's like what do they want&nbsp;
and the writer looks at me like I have no idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:35.840
like they've written a whole script and they're&nbsp;
just they still kind of don't understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:43.520
what the main character wants and so when someone&nbsp;
is thinking about their their main character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.160
you know I always like to break down what they&nbsp;
want into two parts like they have an emotional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:54.800
need like to find love or connection or for&nbsp;
revenge or power or whatever and then there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.800 --> 00:02:59.600
a specific immediate goal that they pursue in&nbsp;
the story that satisfies that emotional need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:06.480
so if you don't have those two things and know&nbsp;
them pretty clearly you're probably going to write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.920
a lot of pages that you don't end up having&nbsp;
in your final version of the script because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.200
you know I don't I don't know about you but&nbsp;
i've written full scripts where I literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:20.960
have no idea what they want and this happens this&nbsp;
happens this happens and then I look at the script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:26.480
and i'm like what is this like what is this thing&nbsp;
why should I be invested in watching this story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:33.280
so I think a way to know if you if your&nbsp;
character has enough of an engine to drive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:39.120
a feature or a television show it's like they&nbsp;
have to have a very specific emotional need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:45.360
and then they have a very specific goal that&nbsp;
they're pursuing in the story to satisfy that need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:50.960
now when they start the movie or the tv show that&nbsp;
immediate goal that they're pursuing could be like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:59.520
the wrong thing right and their goal changes&nbsp;
but usually the emotional need stays constant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:05.520
as they sort of grow then they may change that&nbsp;
that immediate goal to kind of reveal their their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.520 --> 00:04:11.520
growth as a character or whatever but like you&nbsp;
know do you know what your character emotionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:17.600
needs and wants and then what is it that&nbsp;
they specifically decide to do to satisfy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:22.080
that need and once you have that lined up then&nbsp;
you're good you're good to go and start writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:30.960
with conflict how do we know how much house&nbsp;
like is it too much too soon or there can never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:35.520
be too much conflict too soon like you know some&nbsp;
people think they have conflict in their story and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:41.040
it's actually not it's it's too it's too meek&nbsp;
it's not it's not enough right that's a great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:52.080
question about conflict for me like even if you're&nbsp;
writing the most small specific character-driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:59.280
movie that takes place in a very kind of&nbsp;
small location with just a few characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.840 --> 00:05:05.200
there has to be lots of conflict there needs&nbsp;
to be internal conflict and external conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.200 --> 00:05:10.560
of some kind however that doesn't mean your&nbsp;
your movie has to start with like conflict&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:16.000
conflict conflict conflict too much conflict&nbsp;
because after a while it's like whoah what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:23.520
this what's happening here what's this about so&nbsp;
one way to sort of think about conflict and how it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:27.760
works especially when you're when you're writing a&nbsp;
feature film is that you know at the beginning of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:33.200
the story your character may want something that&nbsp;
they don't have right and they're just kind of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:38.000
living their life but we get the sense that they&nbsp;
want something that they don't have and then there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:43.760
is this inciting incident or call to adventure&nbsp;
where maybe they have the chance to get what they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:49.280
want but it's like super scary right like there's&nbsp;
something emotionally dangerous about that call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:56.000
or physically dangerous and so they go nope i'm&nbsp;
going to refuse that call because i'm too afraid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:01.440
and then something usually happens to kind&nbsp;
of force the person to take action and heed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:06.000
the call to adventure and go on the journey&nbsp;
at the end of act one so it's not like you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.040
have to have conflict conflict you know it's&nbsp;
like like usually most really great stories&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:15.600
depending on what genre you're writing in I mean&nbsp;
if you're writing an action movie there should be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:20.880
something big up top right and then it creates&nbsp;
a question we're like what happened and then we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:24.880
slow down and we start to go meet up with the&nbsp;
character and what they're trying to figure out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.160 --> 00:06:32.000
so you can have sections of your script that are&nbsp;
quiet where we're just kind of revealing character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.000 --> 00:06:35.680
and they're still trying to get what they want&nbsp;
but the conflicts are smaller and more internal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.000
but in the climax of your movie whether you're&nbsp;
writing a tiny movie or you're writing a huge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:46.560
movie you know where there's car chases&nbsp;
and explosions or whatever that is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:53.280
moment where there has to be big conflict either&nbsp;
emotionally or physically so conflict is something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.000 --> 00:06:59.600
that you have to kind of modulate throughout the&nbsp;
script right and there are moments in the story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.600 --> 00:07:04.560
that are quieter where maybe we get in act two we&nbsp;
have a moment where everything kind of slows down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:10.400
and we have a scene with two characters connecting&nbsp;
on a deeper emotional level that then create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.400 --> 00:07:16.320
bigger stakes so that when they move into the next&nbsp;
